the doom metal genre."}], "places": [{"name": "Birmingham, England", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Worcester, Massachusetts", "role": "Major festival location"}, {"name": "Stockholm, Sweden", "role": "Influential scene"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: doom metal", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "doom-metal"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q186170", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_metal"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "doom metal contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Doom metal significantly shaped the evolution of heavy metal, introducing themes of despair and existentialism that resonate with contemporary societal issues.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:05Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Doom metal is an extreme subgenre of heavy metal music that typically uses slower tempos, low-tuned guitars and a much "thicker" or "heavier" sound than other heavy metal genres. Both the music and the lyrics are intended to evoke a sense of despair, dread, and impending doom. The genre is strongly influenced by the early work of Black Sabbath, who formed a prototype for doom metal. During the first half of the 1980s, a number of bands such as Witchfinder General and Pagan Altar from England, American bands Pentagram, Saint Vitus, the Obsessed, Trouble, and Cirith Ungol, and Swedish band Candlemass defined doom metal as a distinct genre.", "newValue": "Doom metal emerged in the late 1960s and early 1970s as an extreme subgenre of heavy metal music, characterized by its slow tempos, low-tuned guitars, and a sound that evokes despair and dread. Its roots lie in Birmingham, England, where Black Sabbath, formed in 1968, crafted a sound that would lay the groundwork for doom metal. This genre has gained a global following, influencing numerous bands and subcultures, and it remains significant in the larger context of heavy metal history. Pioneers such as Pentagram, Saint Vitus, and Candlemass further developed the genre, solidifying its identity and thematic depth.

The 1980s saw the rise of several influential doom metal bands, such as Witchfinder General and Pagan Altar in England, as well as the emergence of the American doom scene with bands like Trouble and The Obsessed. These groups often embraced themes of horror, existential dread, and the occult, diverging from the more glam-oriented aspects of heavy metal prevalent at the time. The genre experienced a resurgence in the 1990s with the advent of stoner rock and the incorporation of sludge metal elements, leading to a broader acceptance of heavier sounds across the metal spectrum. Key festivals and gatherings began to celebrate doom metal, creating a community around the genre.

Doom metal's legacy is profound, influencing a wide array of subgenres and fostering the growth of underground music scenes globally. It has inspired countless musicians and brought attention to themes of mental health, existential crises, and societal issues through its lyrics. The genre's direct impact can be seen in the formation of various festivals and the establishment of dedicated record labels that champion doom and its offshoots. As stated by musician Scott 'Wino' Weinrich, 'Doom is about the weight of the world on your shoulders, and that’s something we all feel.'"}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:05Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The heavy metal movement of the late 1960s provided a foundation for the emergence of doom metal.", "Black Sabbath's early work established a prototype for slower, heavier music characterized by themes of despair.", "The cultural climate of the 1980s, marked by social unrest and existential angst, fostered the growth of darker music genres."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:05Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["Doom metal influenced the development of various subgenres, including stoner rock and sludge metal.", "It created a global community of fans and musicians dedicated to exploring heavy, thematic music.", "The genre has contributed to discussions on mental health and existential themes in contemporary music culture."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T17:39:05Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27707702015", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "doom-metal", "sourceName": "doom metal", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-contemporary", "targetName": "Contemporary", "context": "doom metal is situated within the Contemporary (960)